<h1>app.Map() Middleware in ASP.NET Core</h1>

<p>
    <strong>app.Map()</strong> is used to create a separate middleware pipeline
    based on a specific URL path.
</p>

<p>
    It allows ASP.NET Core applications to branch the request pipeline
    for different routes.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Basic Syntax</h2>

<pre>
<code>
app.Map("/admin", adminApp =>
{
    adminApp.Run(async context =>
    {
        await context.Response.WriteAsync("Welcome to Admin Panel");
    });
});
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>How app.Map() Works</h2>

<ul>
    <li>ASP.NET Core checks the incoming request URL.</li>
    <li>If the URL matches the specified path, request enters the mapped branch.</li>
    <li>A separate middleware pipeline executes for that branch.</li>
    <li>If the URL does not match, the normal pipeline continues.</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Important Point</h2>

<p>
    <strong>app.Map()</strong> creates a completely separate middleware branch
    for a specific request path.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Example 1: Admin Route Branch</h2>

<pre>
<code>
app.Map("/admin", adminApp =>
{
    adminApp.Run(async context =>
    {
        await context.Response.WriteAsync("Welcome to Admin Panel");
    });
});
</code>
</pre>

<h3>URL</h3>

<pre>
<code>
https://localhost:xxxx/admin
</code>
</pre>

<h3>Output</h3>

<pre>
<code>
Welcome to Admin Panel
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Internal Request Flow</h2>

<pre>
<code>
Client Request
       ↓
Check URL Path
       ↓
/admin Matched
       ↓
Admin Middleware Branch
       ↓
Response Returned
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Difference Between app.Use() and app.Map()</h2>

<table border="1" cellpadding="10">

    <tr>
        <th>Feature</th>
        <th>app.Use()</th>
        <th>app.Map()</th>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Purpose</td>
        <td>Add Middleware</td>
        <td>Create Middleware Branch</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Works Based On</td>
        <td>Pipeline Order</td>
        <td>URL Path</td>
    </tr>

    <tr>
        <td>Creates Separate Pipeline</td>
        <td>No</td>
        <td>Yes</td>
    </tr>

</table>

<hr />

<h2>Real World Use Case: Admin Panel Middleware</h2>

<p>
    In real applications, app.Map() is commonly used to create
    separate pipelines for:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Admin Panels</li>
    <li>API Versioning</li>
    <li>Tenant-Based Applications</li>
    <li>Health Check Endpoints</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h3>Step 1: Open Program.cs File</h3>

<p>
    Open the <strong>Program.cs</strong> file of your ASP.NET Core MVC project.
</p>

<hr />

<h3>Step 2: Add app.Map() Middleware</h3>

<p>
    Add the following code before:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.MapControllerRoute(
</code>
</pre>

<p>
    Add this middleware:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
app.Map("/admin", adminApp =>
{
    adminApp.Run(async context =>
    {
        context.Response.ContentType = "text/html";

        await context.Response.WriteAsync("
            &lt;h1&gt;Admin Dashboard&lt;/h1&gt;
            &lt;p&gt;Welcome to the Admin Panel.&lt;/p&gt;
        ");
    });
});
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Step 3: Run the Application</h3>

<p>
    Run the application and open:
</p>

<pre>
<code>
https://localhost:xxxx/admin
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h3>Browser Output</h3>

<div style="border:1px solid #ccc; padding:20px; margin-top:10px;">

    <h1>Admin Dashboard</h1>

    <p>Welcome to the Admin Panel.</p>

</div>

<hr />

<h3>Important Observation</h3>

<p>
    Only requests starting with <strong>/admin</strong>
    will enter this middleware branch.
</p>

<p>
    All other requests will continue through the normal middleware pipeline.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Point</h2>

<p>
    app.Map() is used to create separate middleware branches in ASP.NET Core
    based on URL paths.
</p>